# Protections for group (a) of the indirect-call-hijack corpus (fnptr_*):
# HOW THE POINTER IS REACHED. Held IDENTICAL across every target in this
# group so that the pointer's location and reachability are the only
# variables.
#
# -fno-stack-protector  a canary is genuinely not in the way of this category:
#                       the hijacked call happens in the middle of the function,
#                       long before any epilogue check. It is disabled so that a
#                       canary cannot be mistaken for the reason a variant fails.
# -no-pie               the payload is an ADDRESS (a win function, or system@plt),
#                       so a fixed image base is what makes this GROUP solvable
#                       without a leak. The leak-first shape is not dropped from
#                       the corpus -- it is group (b)'s objptr_25_pie_leak_first,
#                       which is the ONE target here built -pie, deliberately.
# -O0                   at higher -O the indirect call through a struct member is
#                       devirtualised or the pointer is kept in a register, and the
#                       target silently stops being in this category at all.
-fno-stack-protector
-no-pie
-O0
